f(x, x) := 1 ⇒ error 1:6: the parameters must be different names, as in `f(x, y) := x + y`
f(2) := 1 ⇒ error 1:3: the parameters must be different names, as in `f(x, y) := x + y`
(a := 5) ⇒ error 1:4: a definition with `:=` must be a statement of its own, as in `a := 5`
solve(a := 1, a) ⇒ error 1:9: a definition with `:=` must be a statement of its own, as in `a := 5`
x -> a := x ⇒ error 1:8: a definition with `:=` must be a statement of its own, as in `a := 5`
e := 3 ⇒ error 1:1: `e` is a constant, so it cannot be used as a name here
sin := 2 ⇒ error 1:1: `sin` is built in, so it cannot be used as a name here
if := 1 ⇒ error 1:1: `if` is a reserved word, so it cannot be used as a name here
π := 3 ⇒ error 1:1: `π` is a constant, so it cannot be used as a name here
ℤ := 1 ⇒ error 1:1: `ℤ` is a number domain, so it cannot be used as a name here
Reals := 1 ⇒ error 1:1: `Reals` is a number domain, so it cannot be used as a name here
lim := 1 ⇒ error 1:1: `lim` is a reserved word, so it cannot be used as a name here
f(e) := e + 1 ⇒ f(e) := e + 1
f(x) := x f(x - 1) ⇒ f(x) := x*f(x - 1)
f := x -> x f(x - 1) ⇒ f := x -> x*f(x - 1)
g(x) := f(x) + 1 ⇒ g(x) := f*x + 1
f(t) := t^2\ng(x) := f(x)\nf := 3\nf(2) ⇒ f(t) := t^2 | g(x) := f(x) | f := 3 | f*2
f(t) := t^2\nclear(f)\nf(2) ⇒ f(t) := t^2 | clear(f) | f*2

